From fbc90617593fe01dfa67dd990b1b3d5fbc86f117 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:31:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Newton solver: stagnation of the residual accepted at its
 floor

The residual is computed with rounding errors: its norm cannot go below a
floor, which grows with the size of the problem and with the level of the
stresses, and which is much higher in single precision (about 1.6e-5 to
2e-5 of the reference residual for a beam of 111,843 unknowns in small
strain, 3e-4 in finite strain). A relative tolerance below this floor
can't be reached: in float, the Newton solver of the beam stopped after 50
iterations without convergence with the criterion of the case (1e-8).

New option StagnationRelativeTolerance of the Newton solver: below this
relative residual, a residual whose smallest value has not been halved
during the last two iterations is accepted as converged, with a message.
Its default value is 1e4 times the machine epsilon of the numerical type
(about 1.2e-3 with float, 2.2e-12 with double); a null value disables it.
With float, the beam now converges in 27 iterations, 7.0e-5 away from the
solution in double (8.0e-5 with a criterion of 1e-4). With double, the
criterion is not triggered by the test suites of mfem-mgis, of the
examples and of mm-opera-hpc, whose results are the same bit for bit.

Tests (float only, where the floor is reached in a few iterations; in
double, the residual of these tests keeps decreasing slowly): with a
relative tolerance of 1e-30, UniaxialTensileTest converges with the
stagnation criterion and fails without it. UniaxialTensileTest gets the
options --relative-tolerance and --stagnation-relative-tolerance, and ends
with a failure, not an abort, when a resolution does not converge.
Documentation in the user guide (Newton algorithm) and in the installation
guide.
